# Project map

This file maps each folder you work in to its project and its KB. It stays on this machine: git ignores `projects-mapping.md`.

- Copy this file to `projects-mapping.md` in the harness main checkout, then add one row per folder.
- `scripts/project-map.py` reads the first table. The status line, the folders pane, the project start and the `/add-dir` hook read it through that script.
- After each git commit, the `project-map-check` hook names a folder that is on no row.
- Give each folder that has a `.claude/` folder at its top its own row. Claude Code loads skills only from `.claude/skills/` at the top of an added folder, so a git repo is not what counts.
- The Role is one of `frontend`, `backend`, `mobile`, `infra`, `lib` (code: these show under the status line), `KB` or `tooling`. The status line shows a role off the list on a red tag.

| Project | Folder | Role | KB | Note |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| harness | ~/second-brain/harness | tooling | personal | the skills, hooks and settings that each KB shares |

## Domain prompts

A pick of a domain in `/domains` runs `/add-dir` for its folders, then puts the text under its `### <domain>` heading in the prompt box. Keep the Context first and the "What I want to run" line last: the cursor lands at the end of the text, so you type the task there. A domain with no heading gets no prompt.

### example-domain

Context:
- <folder 1> is ...
- <folder 2> is ...

What I want to run (blank means context only: confirm and wait):
